r/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543078} {:answer/id #uuid "b67ada9d-787b-5291-af4f-7eef69f5678d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543114} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ede49-5f0c-5bd8-a8bd-3022152fe7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b67ada9d-787b-5291-af4f-7eef69f5678d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd04507-0815-5cf4-b8b9-c2671207a55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "841f3191-5c7e-5e47-b725-127033cb9a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-79453" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb557ae-9a47-526e-ae26-b4f9d80275d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542726} {:answer/id #uuid "a31007c0-bb7e-5195-b1fe-bac89c68dbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543070} {:answer/id #uuid "cff918bc-b5d0-5906-8d66-bbe4168f3474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543178} {:answer/id #uuid "daad00e3-599f-5edb-abd8-30682e338221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543199} {:answer/id #uuid "de2a4dff-046b-5136-b0e7-22b11eceaca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31007c0-bb7e-5195-b1fe-bac89c68dbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543070} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aab8c1f-76ff-5a2c-9c46-d7294b8c857e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543330}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9796dfce-fb6b-57bd-b442-e9278daaaa20" :question/math-academy-id "q-98925" :question/problem "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba57988-9002-55a9-90cb-d6888023eb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656325} {:answer/id #uuid "28b16e8a-7e2b-54b0-8fc1-d35ecc2d61ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656329} {:answer/id #uuid "8169a85e-eb7a-5c34-9cb3-68cfa6f1ebbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656343} {:answer/id #uuid "acd95844-f8e1-5ab3-8022-2795721f5653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656348} {:answer/id #uuid "f399d004-4fb7-595d-b566-82e031606d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acd95844-f8e1-5ab3-8022-2795721f5653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656348} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0775bf8-2103-5609-b150-1b6fc2e8a958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656347}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31f7c11e-ddce-52f3-9df1-4a9dbff26cc2" :question/problem "If $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=5$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=5$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 4=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=22.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f65777d-80f7-5e88-a5e4-42ab702c4e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656326} {:answer/id #uuid "46624c46-3543-55f5-8f52-a8707e99b9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656334} {:answer/id #uuid "663d9a2e-6940-50df-bd10-d976166405db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656337} {:answer/id #uuid "76ece62c-ce60-569b-9a0e-3c322a1cf0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656340} {:answer/id #uuid "befe5896-389c-569b-b84d-45d2c3144414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "663d9a2e-6940-50df-bd10-d976166405db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59222b9-b874-5166-afe6-714c00276c20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "382ed20b-83b3-5caf-8ca1-a3baabb17216" :question/problem "If $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=-2$, then $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=-2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02672d6a-6b09-5256-a2a9-8c433d6cf9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656324} {:answer/id #uuid "15a2f4e5-6249-5bd1-be1a-c260c42fad17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656327} {:answer/id #uuid "76535ebc-b6b2-5fab-bce3-525acccc81b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656339} {:answer/id #uuid "926b2305-5427-5cb4-bd90-523bbf663857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656346} {:answer/id #uuid "f31c7c54-b5ce-5646-a6f2-42af59004b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02672d6a-6b09-5256-a2a9-8c433d6cf9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656324} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb7d791-b1a7-5cdf-8702-c1d66906b072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656335}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a155d3c-0437-5bdf-afb1-a5fd32a1ba61" :question/problem "If $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=4$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{4}\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=4$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 5=40.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)=40.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f35321b-ebb6-5b19-8034-e3adaf3e93c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656328} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6fa17e-2ce8-5435-b086-2c5c2b2f576e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656330} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1ec20a-1204-5bd9-811d-b421989c9e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656342} {:answer/id #uuid "c623e038-ece1-5c3a-b8a1-6c63962d212f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656353} {:answer/id #uuid "eba6ce4e-9667-5721-9a14-4d9b87d68e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f1ec20a-1204-5bd9-811d-b421989c9e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656342} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2837859-88f8-5f3d-b770-7b6159f0de3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ba7524-c55c-55c4-befa-65fc65e1d586" :question/problem "If $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, then $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right) &=\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot 5+k=k+25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=k+25.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31394b5a-0a89-50ee-a198-98ed5f8215e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656331} {:answer/id #uuid "6070547e-e428-529e-a84b-1df9799fa82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656336} {:answer/id #uuid "8b536607-fa87-5ffd-8d2a-cbd2339f179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656345} {:answer/id #uuid "b78bd294-14c4-5e96-b82c-9bb5132bd072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656350} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d5a6b7-ce02-5c4e-9888-7cb1f9950dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6070547e-e428-529e-a84b-1df9799fa82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656336} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a715ff5-d21b-522c-a600-5e9414794bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbf69bc5-72a9-5eeb-b7a8-7c8cd5feae2d" :question/problem "If $\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)=-5$, then $\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right) &=\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}\\right)+\\log_{10}\\left(y^{5}\\right) \\\\ &=3\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)=-5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=3\\cdot (-5)+5\\cdot 6=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)=15.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Power Rules of Logarithms"}]}
